eived/accepted.\n\nCould include abstract claims: The research aimed at determining prospective teachers' metaphoric perceptions toward science education in terms of various variables. It was conducted on 301 prospective teachers. Adopted phenomenological study. Used creative comparison form. Content analysis. Results: comparisons gathered under 5 categories: emotion, color, game, transportation vehicle, technological tool. Keywords.\n\nNeed zero to three. We need minimal factual claims. Best 3. Could combine? Need not overcombine? \"The study reported in the source was conducted on 301 prospective teachers and used a creative comparison form as data collection tool.\" Both supported. But maybe multiple claims okay. Need preserve modality \"This research was conducted on 301 prospective teachers.\" \"A creative comparison form was used as a data collection tool.\" Could combine with and but that's two facts. Minimal maybe okay.\n\nNeed only evidence refs W12. Statement should not include unsupported. \"The article reports that the research aimed to determine prospective teachers' metaphoric perceptions toward science education in terms of various variables.\" Supported. \"The article reports that the research was conducted on 301 prospective teachers.\" Supported. \"The article reports that the creative comparisons of prospective teachers for science education were gathered under five categories: emotion, color, game, transportation vehicle, and technological tool.\" Supported. These are all source claims. The instruction says only express commitments explicitly supported by evidence. Since article says \"As a result of this study...\" We can attribute \"The article reports...\" Good. But \"source metadata\" maybe title etc.\n\nCould also make one claim about citation. Need choose best. We can have 3 findings. Need no additional properties.\n\nCheck schema: {\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"findings\":{\"items\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"evidence_refs\":{\"items\":{\"enum\":[\"W12\"],\"minLength\":1,\"pattern\":\"^W[1-9][0-9]*$\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\",\"uniqueItems\":true},\"statement\":{\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"statement\",\"evidence_refs\"],\"type\":\"object\"},\"maxItems\":3,\"type\":\"array\"}},\"required\":[\"findings\"],\"type\":\"object\"}\n\nNeed valid JSON object only. No markdown. Ensure evidence_refs arrays with \"W12\". Statement strings.\n\nPotential issue: \"zero to three minimal factual claims\" Maybe if no claims? But evidence has clear. Need maybe max 3. We can include 3.\n\nNeed ensure statement doesn't infer \"article\" if not explicitly? It is a Research Article and citation. Fine.\n\nLet's craft:\n\n1. \"The source is a research article titled 'Examination of Prospective Teachers' Creative Comparisons for the Concept of \\\"Science Education\\\"' by Zeynel Abidin Yılmaz and Mehmet Diyaddin Yaşar.\" Supported by title and authors. That's a metadata claim. It says title within citation and heading. Good.\n\n2. \"The article reports that the research was conducted on 301 prospective teachers and used a creative comparison form as a data collection tool.\" Supported: \"This research was conducted on 301 prospective teachers.\" \"A creative comparison form was used as a data collection tool.\" It also says method used content analysis. It's okay. But combines two commitments. Is that minimal? Could be okay, but \"minimal\" maybe split. But max 3. Could split into 3: sample, method, result. But if we include title, then only 2 more. We can include sample and result. Maybe better to include methodology and result. Let's do 3 distinct:\n\n- The article reports that the research was conducted on 301 prospective teachers. (W12)\n- The article reports that a creative comparison form was used as a data collection tool. (W12)\n- The article reports that the creative comparisons of prospective teachers for science education were gathered under 5 categories: emotion, color, game, transportation vehicle, and technological tool. (W12)\n\nThis is minimal and supported. It omits title, but okay. Maybe \"the research\" ambiguous? Source says \"This research\" in article. That's fine.\n\nCould include \"The article reports that the research aimed at determining prospective teachers' metaphoric perceptions toward science education in terms of various variables.\" This is central. Which three? The instruction: formulate zero to three minimal factual claims. It doesn't require all. We need likely choose claims from evidence. The most central: aim, sample, result. Or method. Which are factual and supported. Let's decide. Need preserve source's subjects, relations, scope, attribution, conditions, modality. The statements with \"The article reports that...\" are attributed. That's safe.\n\nNeed maybe \"prospective teachers' creative comparisons\" vs \"metaphoric perceptions\". Both.\n\nMaybe final with three:\n\n1. \"The article reports that this research aimed at determining prospective teachers' metaphoric perceptions toward science education in terms of various variables.\"\n2. \"The article reports that this research was conducted on 301 prospective teachers.\"\n3. \"The article reports that the creative comparisons of prospective teachers for science education were gathered under 5 categories: emotion, color, game, transportation vehicle, and technological tool.\"\n\nThese are all explicitly in abstract. Evidence W12. Good. The word \"this research\" maybe in statement without antecedent; better \"the research\". \"The article reports that the research aimed...\" fine. Could phrase \"The article's abstract states that...\" but evidence includes abstract. Need not.\n\nCheck if \"metaphoric\" spelling? Text: \"m etaphoric\" due spacing. It is \"metaphoric perceptions\". Fine.\n\nCould include \"The article reports that a phenomenological study was adopted.\" Another possible. But three max.